# 2. DEVELOPMENT RULES

Follow these rules throughout implementation.

1. Implement actual functionality. Never simulate a successful evidence verification or browser extraction.
2. Do not use hardcoded sample events as if they came from real evidence.
3. Keep demonstration data clearly marked as synthetic.
4. Every UI action must have a real backend implementation or be visibly marked as unavailable.
5. Show useful errors when a database is missing, malformed, unsupported, locked, or inaccessible.
6. Never silently discard parsing errors or unsupported records.
7. Preserve source references for extracted artifacts.
8. Keep original evidence separate from working copies.
9. Do not store passwords, cookies, authentication tokens, session secrets, or unrelated sensitive browser information.
10. Do not modify source browser databases to make them easier to parse.
11. Use parameterized SQL queries.
12. Validate all file paths and user-supplied input.
13. Do not use shell execution with untrusted user input.
14. Do not claim that a hash proves evidence authenticity. A hash establishes consistency with the recorded hash at verification time.
15. Make the interface responsive, accessible, polished, and professional.
16. Do not finish with empty modules, TODO-only implementations, fake buttons, or placeholder API responses.
17. If a feature is too large to complete safely, implement a smaller functional version and clearly document the limitation.
18. Never claim tests passed unless they were actually run and their results were inspected.

